{:edb/db-before-t 1108 :edb/db-after-t 1109 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534421 50 #inst "2026-10-07T02:24:50.419Z" 13194139534421 true] [17592186096627 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287422 13194139534421 true] [17592186096627 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287436 13194139534421 true] [17592186096629 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287429 13194139534421 true] [17592186096629 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287439 13194139534421 true] [17592186097028 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534421 true] [17592186097028 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/f2de46decf74a41e6375325e6eeb015113311b00300eb639d0f1f85a9f779337.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534421 true] [17592186097028 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1132/Source/Images/q-14052.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534421 false] [17592186097028 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-5,-6)$ and passes through the point $(-1,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-5,-6)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =4p(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-1,2),$ we substitute $x=-1$ and $y=2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1+5)^{2} & =4p(2+6) \\\\ 16 & =4p(8) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =2(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =2(0+6) \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =12 \\\\ x+5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{12} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}-5.$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287422 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287423 13194139534421 true] [17592186287422 1089 "q-14050" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287422 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534421 true] [17592186287422 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/0470661491c0d9d44ecbbc3c87af70d4ff334f1ffd2ca56b68bd4c126f3d88de.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287422 1113 #uuid "1270303e-0109-58be-ac65-6aecbfb8540f" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287422 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,-4)$ and passes through the point $(0,-\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,-\\frac{7}{4}),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{4}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-3)^{2} & =4p(-\\frac{7}{4}+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(0+4) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $7$ and $-1.$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287423 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287424 13194139534421 true] [17592186287423 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287425 13194139534421 true] [17592186287423 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287426 13194139534421 true] [17592186287423 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287427 13194139534421 true] [17592186287423 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287428 13194139534421 true] [17592186287423 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534421 true] [17592186287423 1041 "selection" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287423 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287425 13194139534421 true] [17592186287423 1110 #uuid "e62ee236-d0f5-56d1-ab73-e2ba41ae5b80" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287424 1034 "$8$ and $-1$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287424 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534421 true] [17592186287424 1111 #uuid "1b34bf13-64ca-557f-a0a2-5636a64c6009" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287425 1034 "$7$ and $-1$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287425 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534421 true] [17592186287425 1111 #uuid "b917d03e-e0f2-5f50-b6b3-8fcee0175693" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287426 1034 "$9$ and $-2$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287426 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534421 true] [17592186287426 1111 #uuid "473f69b9-9812-5ee9-a288-68c3ed1d0bbd" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287427 1034 "$7$ and $-2$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287427 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534421 true] [17592186287427 1111 #uuid "33b53780-aa11-56b8-9508-05c03d806b58" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287428 1034 "$8$ and $-3$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287428 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534421 true] [17592186287428 1111 #uuid "684e3212-593c-5c35-887a-80e212357ba4" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287429 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287430 13194139534421 true] [17592186287429 1089 "q-14241" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287429 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534421 true] [17592186287429 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/9b7acbd4f24b6bbd20dd00fc042405324c069d28db0102c2cd7dd49e6b3bbccd.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287429 1113 #uuid "0ecc876e-1e30-5d04-b02b-e0d45341e6f7" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287429 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,4)$ and passes through the point $(-6,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(2,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}=4p(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,8),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=8$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8-4)^{2} & =4p(-6-2) \\\\ 16 & =4p(-8) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =-2(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =-2(0-2) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2+4 \\\\ y & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $2$ and  $6.$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287431 13194139534421 true] [17592186287430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287432 13194139534421 true] [17592186287430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287433 13194139534421 true] [17592186287430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287434 13194139534421 true] [17592186287430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287435 13194139534421 true] [17592186287430 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534421 true] [17592186287430 1041 "selection" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287430 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287432 13194139534421 true] [17592186287430 1110 #uuid "6342a59e-8cce-585f-b1a4-39040a9eef59" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287431 1034 "$2$ and $7$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287431 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534421 true] [17592186287431 1111 #uuid "38592871-f043-5184-b2b4-327ef67c82d6" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287432 1034 "$2$ and $6$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287432 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534421 true] [17592186287432 1111 #uuid "12257405-0e0c-5f7d-83b3-2dbf478154da" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287433 1034 "$1$ and $6$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287433 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534421 true] [17592186287433 1111 #uuid "6b453dca-9ef9-5131-b9a1-3138abe80db2" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287434 1034 "$3$ and $5$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287434 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534421 true] [17592186287434 1111 #uuid "a400110b-5e31-5036-98bb-f3b44593a7a6" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287435 1034 "$1$ and $7$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287435 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534421 true] [17592186287435 1111 #uuid "440caec2-dec0-51d1-b80b-187b7d5464a3" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287436 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287437 13194139534421 true] [17592186287436 1089 "q-251692" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287436 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534421 true] [17592186287436 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/8b48ef824df51aa680a727dc46d04e0936f61729475026b90378e153db503c76.png)\n\nThe value of the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above is{{field-1}}." 13194139534421 true] [17592186287436 1113 #uuid "72b27de8-4c9e-5d25-95fa-3b9ff814dba0" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287436 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-6,2)$ and passes through the point $(-7,4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-6,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-2)^{2}=4p(x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-7,4),$ we substitute $x=-7$ and $y=4$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-2)^{2} & =4p(-7+6) \\\\ (2)^{2} & =4p\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 4 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =-4(x+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+2)^{2} & =-4(x+6) \\\\ 4 & =-4(x+6) \\\\ -\\frac{4}{4} & =x+6 \\\\ x & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-7.$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287437 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287438 13194139534421 true] [17592186287437 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534421 true] [17592186287437 1041 "field-1" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287437 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287438 13194139534421 true] [17592186287437 1110 #uuid "b0fa099f-8147-557e-813d-150ebc4c0b8e" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287438 1034 "-7" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287438 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534421 true] [17592186287438 1111 #uuid "e136ee47-27fc-560d-aee3-61dca23f5f29" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287439 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287440 13194139534421 true] [17592186287439 1089 "q-251800" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287439 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534421 true] [17592186287439 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/b03beb34efa3fef7aaf18a879eb628748d86dc1b47eb4b45f60c4d02935e372b.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." 13194139534421 true] [17592186287439 1113 #uuid "e981b6dc-b9b2-5bb8-9cef-bfdc3600a8d8" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287439 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(4,-3)$ and passes through the point $(10,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(4,-3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+3).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(10,6),$ we substitute $x=10$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10-4)^{2} & =4p(6+3) \\\\ 36 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =4(y+3) \\\\ 16 & =4(y+3) \\\\ 4 & =y+3 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $1.$" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287441 13194139534421 true] [17592186287440 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534421 true] [17592186287440 1041 "field-1" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287440 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287441 13194139534421 true] [17592186287440 1110 #uuid "17c2a04e-38dc-5402-9867-7517c2eeb3ea" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287441 1034 "1" 13194139534421 true] [17592186287441 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534421 true] [17592186287441 1111 #uuid "879bf4d5-5247-5f7b-8a67-61010f549506" 13194139534421 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-14050" 17592186287422 "q-14050/selection" 17592186287423 "q-14050/selection/4836b28dd052c2d7da63abac5ad842fb7a92dc6fee04b4c2c148f88419b7c81a" 17592186287424 "q-14050/selection/8661436cfd746f4b60cd5691f6ee5f8a7b3f9f18a1f8541065c1a58346bad80d" 17592186287425 "q-14050/selection/c5564736bf65a0aea6d4e1707a2d0a41d80abe542754349572e910163901aa31" 17592186287426 "q-14050/selection/f05e363b4806ff9170bffb969ac17a278512549bf0f5a380a1917fe4214fff42" 17592186287427 "q-14050/selection/faec1baa4968c88a7613e14a58575a631b79203d9aee2bbfdbbd42fbdd23d23b" 17592186287428 "q-14241" 17592186287429 "q-14241/selection" 17592186287430 "q-14241/selection/36a80e34ea956915fe307f304767a10300947e44666f1b86d04fb9e41ad06177" 17592186287431 "q-14241/selection/7a8a4a2a5bacf349af12b1dfa4cdf0eff32871c3c2fab8b03119314869f88d1d" 17592186287432 "q-14241/selection/aab1ad9ce321e6a6425295ccc726ed413e7f7f2637636c7d12fc5a2d89502fb5" 17592186287433 "q-14241/selection/cecc18c19de4b1bd11